WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.990 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  a seismic shift in climate science  might be heating up new research shows  that sound waves produced by earthquakes  can be used to measure temperatures in  the ocean which traps 90  of the heat earth absorbs from the sun  making long-term changes in ocean warmth  a major factor in how the world might  respond to global warming  for years the mainstay of measuring  ocean temperature has been argo  an array of 4 000 autonomous floats  which drifts the globe  sampling ocean water and measuring its  temperature  yet argo measurements stop at 2 000  meters  the new technique called seismic ocean  thermometry  would be especially useful in detecting  long-term changes in ocean temperatures  deeper than argos reach ocean acoustic  tomography  the basis for the current research was  first tested  nearly 30 years ago the initial studies  created sound waves artificially  basically cranking the volume on giant  underwater speakers  scientists measured the sound's travel  time from the speakers  to receivers thousands of kilometers  away because ocean temperatures affect  the speed of the waves  the researchers could calculate average  temperatures along their paths  but some believe the noise was a threat  to ocean life  and the technique never took off  the new study instead uses a natural  sound source for investigation  earthquakes rumbling beneath the  seafloor off the coast of sumatra  that drum up acoustic waves in the ocean  on the shores of the kagos islands in  the east indian ocean  between 2005 and 2016  seismic station diego garcia recorded  seismic waves  produced by those earthquakes some of  those waves created physical changes  in land and sea as they traveled others  were acoustic waves  or t waves that rippled through the deep  ocean  enroute to delivering valuable data  about ocean temperature  the researchers focused on repeaters  aftershocks with nearly identical  origins  which travel one after the other  by keeping the origin of the waves  relatively constant  repeaters helped the researchers home in  on changes in t-wave travel times  in turn this helped them calculate  changes in ocean temperature  some as subtle as thousands of a degree  celsius  twelve years of data coupled with  mathematical models  pointed to a temperature change of  roughly .04  degrees per decade a trend larger than  those predicted by argo  the finding suggests that seismic ocean  thermometry  is a viable method to measure changes in  ocean temperature  further data from other regions of the  globe and other time frames  would help refine the warming models and  bolster projections  and in future studies the researchers  plan to listen  directly for acoustic waves using a  network of hydrophones  first intended to monitor for nuclear  explosions  acoustic waves set the tone for a deep  dive into our warming oceans  even if they fail to reach 20 000  leagues  under the sea

